1
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,600
What does one row mean after a join? Joins can drop rows, keep them, or multiply them.

2
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:09,500
Every ticket paired with every user: the Cartesian product.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:12,500
12 tickets × 8 users = 96 candidate pairs. Most of them are nonsense.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:17,200
The join condition keeps only pairs where the assignee is that user.

5
00:00:17,700 --> 00:00:19,700
Columns 1004 and 1009 stay empty: NULL equals no user.

6
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:22,700
Leave out the condition and all 96 pairs come back. Joins use the condition you write.

7
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:25,200
This is the meaning. PostgreSQL does not have to build all 96 pairs to compute it.

8
00:00:29,900 --> 00:00:34,600
Inner join: only matching pairs. Two real requests silently disappear.

9
00:00:35,100 --> 00:00:37,100
Left join: every ticket on the left side stays. No match? The user columns are NULL.

10
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:39,600
No fake "Unassigned" user, no edited rows. The fix is the join type.

11
00:00:40,100 --> 00:00:42,100
Neither is wrong in general: the join type is a statement about the question.

12
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:48,600
Each ticket pairs with every one of its events.

13
00:00:49,100 --> 00:00:54,100
Ticket 1003 has three events, so it appears in three result rows.

14
00:00:54,600 --> 00:00:57,100
After this join, a row is a ticket-event pair. count(*) counts 21 pairs.

15
00:00:57,600 --> 00:01:00,600
Counting distinct ticket IDs answers a different question: 12 tickets.

16
00:01:01,100 --> 00:01:03,600
Decide what one row means first. DISTINCT does not repair a wrong grain.

17
00:01:06,200 --> 00:01:16,200
Before you count, ask what one row means: A join is a filtered product: the ON condition decides which pairs survive. INNER JOIN drops tickets whose assignee is NULL: 10 rows, not 12. LEFT JOIN keeps every ticket and fills the missing side with NULL. Joining events makes one row per ticket-event pair: 21 rows. Repeated IDs are not automatically a bug. Check the grain first.

